        "sections": [
            {
                "letter": "A",
                "name": "Multiple Choice Questions",
                "marks": 30,
                "questions": [
                    {"stem": "Enalapril is a prodrug because it:", "options": ["Requires hepatic hydrolysis to enalaprilat for ACE inhibition", "Directly blocks angiotensin II receptors", "Is metabolised by CYP3A4 to an active form", "Is converted in the plasma by esterases"], "answer": "Requires hepatic hydrolysis to enalaprilat for ACE inhibition", "explanation": "Enalapril is hydrolysed in the liver to enalaprilat, the active ACE inhibitor. Enalaprilat itself is poorly absorbed orally."},
                    {"stem": "Dipivefrine is a prodrug of epinephrine used in glaucoma because it:", "options": ["Penetrates the cornea better than epinephrine and is hydrolysed intraocularly", "Is more water-soluble than epinephrine", "Has a shorter duration of action than epinephrine", "Does not require activation"], "answer": "Penetrates the cornea better than epinephrine and is hydrolysed intraocularly", "explanation": "Dipivefrine is a lipophilic prodrug that penetrates the cornea 17× better than epinephrine, then is hydrolysed in the aqueous humour."},
                    {"stem": "Mercaptopurine is converted to its active metabolite 6-thioinosinic acid by:", "options": ["Hypoxanthine-guanine phosphoribosyltransferase (HGPRT)", "Xanthine oxidase", "Thiopurine methyltransferase", "Dihydropyrimidine dehydrogenase"], "answer": "Hypoxanthine-guanine phosphoribosyltransferase (HGPRT)", "explanation": "Mercaptopurine requires HGPRT for activation. Allopurinol inhibits xanthine oxidase, a different enzyme, necessitating dose reduction."},
                    {"stem": "Ketoconazole is a potent CYP3A4 inhibitor. Concomitant use with terfenadine caused:", "options": ["QT prolongation and torsades de pointes from elevated terfenadine levels", "Reduced terfenadine efficacy from increased metabolism", "No significant interaction", "Increased renal clearance of terfenadine"], "answer": "QT prolongation and torsades de pointes from elevated terfenadine levels", "explanation": "Ketoconazole inhibits terfenadine metabolism via CYP3A4, causing accumulation and cardiac potassium channel blockade leading to fatal arrhythmias."},
                    {"stem": "Cimetidine inhibits CYP450. Which H2RA is safest to co-prescribe with theophylline?", "options": ["Ranitidine", "Cimetidine", "Famotidine", "Nizatidine"], "answer": "Famotidine", "explanation": "Cimetidine inhibits CYP1A2 (theophylline metabolism). Ranitidine has minimal interaction. Famotidine and nizatidine have no significant CYP inhibition."},
                    {"stem": "Rifampicin reduces the efficacy of oral contraceptives because it:", "options": ["Induces CYP3A4, accelerating ethinyloestradiol and progestin metabolism", "Inhibits oestrogen absorption from the gut", "Displaces oestrogen from sex hormone-binding globulin", "Increases renal clearance of contraceptive hormones"], "answer": "Induces CYP3A4, accelerating ethinyloestradiol and progestin metabolism", "explanation": "Rifampicin is a potent CYP3A4 inducer. Women should use non-hormonal contraception during rifampicin therapy."},
                    {"stem": "Phenytoin is a CYP450 inducer. When co-prescribed with warfarin, the expected effect is:", "options": ["Decreased INR requiring increased warfarin dose", "Increased INR requiring decreased warfarin dose", "No change in anticoagulation", "Increased risk of warfarin-induced bleeding"], "answer": "Decreased INR requiring increased warfarin dose", "explanation": "Phenytoin induces CYP2C9, accelerating warfarin metabolism and reducing INR. Dose escalation of warfarin is typically needed."},
                    {"stem": "Volume of distribution for water-soluble drugs in neonates is increased because:", "options": ["Total body water constitutes ~75% of body weight in neonates", "Neonates have more adipose tissue than adults", "Neonatal plasma protein binding is increased", "Neonatal hepatic metabolism is immature"], "answer": "Total body water constitutes ~75% of body weight in neonates", "explanation": "Neonates have higher total body water (75% vs 60% in adults), increasing Vd for hydrophilic drugs and requiring higher weight-based doses."},
                    {"stem": "In pregnancy, the volume of distribution of most drugs is:", "options": ["Increased due to expanded plasma volume and total body water", "Decreased due to reduced cardiac output", "Unchanged from pre-pregnancy values", "Decreased due to increased albumin binding"], "answer": "Increased due to expanded plasma volume and total body water", "explanation": "Pregnancy increases plasma volume by ~50% and total body water, increasing Vd and often necessitating higher doses for hydrophilic drugs."},
                    {"stem": "A drug with 99% plasma protein binding has a low volume of distribution because:", "options": ["The bound fraction is confined to the vascular compartment", "The drug is actively transported into cells", "The drug is rapidly metabolised in the liver", "The drug is sequestered in adipose tissue"], "answer": "The bound fraction is confined to the vascular compartment", "explanation": "Highly protein-bound drugs remain largely in plasma (Vd ~0.05 L/kg for albumin-bound drugs), limiting tissue distribution."},
                    {"stem": "The short duration of action of thiopentone after a single IV bolus is due to:", "options": ["Redistribution from the brain to skeletal muscle and fat", "Rapid hepatic metabolism to inactive metabolites", "Renal excretion of the unchanged drug", "Enzymatic degradation at the site of action"], "answer": "Redistribution from the brain to skeletal muscle and fat", "explanation": "Thiopentone rapidly moves to the brain (high blood flow) then redistributes to muscle and fat (lower blood flow), terminating CNS effects before significant metabolism occurs."},
                    {"stem": "Slow acetylators of isoniazid are at increased risk of:", "options": ["Peripheral neuropathy due to isoniazid accumulation and pyridoxine depletion", "Hepatotoxicity due to excess acetylhydrazine", "Optic neuritis", "Hypersensitivity reactions"], "answer": "Peripheral neuropathy due to isoniazid accumulation and pyridoxine depletion", "explanation": "Slow acetylators have reduced NAT2 activity → higher isoniazid levels → increased pyridoxine excretion → peripheral neuropathy. Prevented with pyridoxine supplementation."},
                    {"stem": "Rapid acetylators of isoniazid are at increased risk of:", "options": ["Hepatotoxicity from acetylhydrazine formation", "Peripheral neuropathy", "Drug-induced lupus", "Stevens-Johnson syndrome"], "answer": "Hepatotoxicity from acetylhydrazine formation", "explanation": "Rapid acetylators produce more acetylisoniazid which is hydrolysed to acetylhydrazine, a hepatotoxic metabolite. This is more common in Asian populations."},
                    {"stem": "The purpose of a loading dose is to:", "options": ["Rapidly achieve the target steady-state plasma concentration", "Reduce the dosing interval for maintenance therapy", "Minimise the risk of adverse drug reactions", "Compensate for poor oral bioavailability"], "answer": "Rapidly achieve the target steady-state plasma concentration", "explanation": "Loading dose = Vd × target Cp. It quickly fills the Vd without waiting for 5 half-lives to reach steady state."},
                    {"stem": "Clearance of a drug is best defined as the:", "options": ["Volume of plasma cleared of drug per unit time", "Amount of drug eliminated per unit time", "Time for drug concentration to halve", "Fraction of dose reaching systemic circulation"], "answer": "Volume of plasma cleared of drug per unit time", "explanation": "Clearance (CL) describes the efficiency of elimination. CL = k × Vd = 0.693 × Vd / t½. Maintenance dose = CL × target Cp."},
                    {"stem": "After 4 half-lives of a drug following first-order kinetics, what percentage has been eliminated?", "options": ["93.75%", "50%", "75%", "87.5%"], "answer": "93.75%", "explanation": "After n half-lives, fraction eliminated = 1 - (½)^n. For n=4: 1 - (½)^4 = 1 - 1/16 = 0.9375 = 93.75%."},
                    {"stem": "Sublingual administration avoids first-pass metabolism because:", "options": ["Venous drainage from the oral cavity bypasses the portal vein", "The sublingual mucosa lacks CYP450 enzymes", "Drugs absorbed sublingually enter the lymphatic system", "Saliva inactivates drug-metabolising enzymes"], "answer": "Venous drainage from the oral cavity bypasses the portal vein", "explanation": "Sublingual veins drain into the superior vena cava via the internal jugular vein, completely bypassing hepatic first-pass metabolism."},
                    {"stem": "Grapefruit juice increases the oral bioavailability of felodipine by:", "options": ["Irreversibly inhibiting intestinal CYP3A4", "Inducing P-glycoprotein in enterocytes", "Increasing gastric pH", "Enhancing hepatic blood flow"], "answer": "Irreversibly inhibiting intestinal CYP3A4", "explanation": "Grapefruit juice furanocoumarins irreversibly inhibit intestinal CYP3A4, increasing felodipine bioavailability from ~15% to ~45%."},
                    {"stem": "A weak acid (pKa 4.5) is absorbed most efficiently in the:", "options": ["Stomach at pH 1.5", "Duodenum at pH 6.0", "Jejunum at pH 7.0", "Ileum at pH 7.5"], "answer": "Stomach at pH 1.5", "explanation": "At pH << pKa, weak acids are non-ionised and can passively diffuse across lipid membranes. The stomach's acidic pH favours absorption."},
                    {"stem": "Weak bases are predominantly absorbed in the small intestine because:", "options": ["The alkaline pH favours the non-ionised form", "The acidic pH favours the ionised form", "The small intestine lacks efflux transporters", "Bile salts enhance weak base solubility"], "answer": "The alkaline pH favours the non-ionised form", "explanation": "Weak bases are non-ionised at pH > pKa. The small intestine pH (6-7.5) keeps weak bases in their absorbable non-ionised form."},
                    {"stem": "The half-life of a drug in first-order kinetics is:", "options": ["Constant regardless of dose and concentration", "Dose-dependent and increases with dose", "Inversely proportional to the rate of absorption", "Dependent on the route of administration"], "answer": "Constant regardless of dose and concentration", "explanation": "In first-order kinetics, t½ is a constant. Only changes in clearance or Vd alter t½. t½ = 0.693 × Vd / CL."},
                    {"stem": "Renal impairment affects drug dosing primarily by:", "options": ["Reducing drug clearance, necessitating dose reduction or extended intervals", "Increasing the volume of distribution", "Enhancing hepatic drug metabolism", "Increasing protein binding of acidic drugs"], "answer": "Reducing drug clearance, necessitating dose reduction or extended intervals", "explanation": "For renally cleared drugs, reduced eGFR decreases CL → prolonged t½ → accumulation → toxicity. Maintenance dose must be reduced."},
                    {"stem": "Enterohepatic recirculation results in:", "options": ["Prolonged drug half-life and extended duration of action", "Rapid drug elimination and shorter half-life", "Increased first-pass metabolism", "Complete avoidance of hepatic metabolism"], "answer": "Prolonged drug half-life and extended duration of action", "explanation": "Drug excreted in bile is reabsorbed from the intestine and re-enters the circulation, extending the drug's presence in the body."},
                    {"stem": "Which of the following is a CYP450 inducer?", "options": ["Carbamazepine", "Ketoconazole", "Cimetidine", "Fluconazole"], "answer": "Carbamazepine", "explanation": "Carbamazepine induces CYP3A4, CYP1A2, and CYP2C9. Ketoconazole, cimetidine, and fluconazole are CYP450 inhibitors."},
                    {"stem": "High plasma protein binding results in:", "options": ["Low volume of distribution and prolonged half-life", "High volume of distribution and rapid clearance", "Increased tissue penetration and shorter duration", "Rapid onset of action and quick offset"], "answer": "Low volume of distribution and prolonged half-life", "explanation": "High protein binding keeps the drug in plasma (low Vd) and the bound reservoir slows elimination, prolonging t½."},
                    {"stem": "Phenytoin follows zero-order kinetics at therapeutic concentrations because:", "options": ["CYP2C9 becomes saturated, limiting the rate of metabolism", "It inhibits its own metabolism", "It is extensively protein-bound", "It has a very long half-life"], "answer": "CYP2C9 becomes saturated, limiting the rate of metabolism", "explanation": "At therapeutic doses, phenytoin's CYP2C9 metabolism is near saturation (Vmax). Small dose increases cause disproportionate concentration rises."},
                    {"stem": "Bioavailability is reduced by all of the following EXCEPT:", "options": ["Administration with a high-fat meal that enhances dissolution", "Extensive first-pass metabolism", "P-glycoprotein-mediated efflux in enterocytes", "Poor aqueous solubility of the drug"], "answer": "Administration with a high-fat meal that enhances dissolution", "explanation": "A high-fat meal that improves dissolution increases the rate and extent of absorption, potentially increasing bioavailability."},
                    {"stem": "Total body clearance equals the sum of:", "options": ["Hepatic + renal + other clearance pathways", "Hepatic × renal clearance", "Volume of distribution × half-life", "Dose absorbed × bioavailability"], "answer": "Hepatic + renal + other clearance pathways", "explanation": "Total clearance is additive across eliminating organs. CLtotal = CLhepatic + CLrenal + CLother (e.g., pulmonary, biliary)."},
                    {"stem": "Loading dose calculation primarily depends on:", "options": ["Volume of distribution and desired plasma concentration", "Clearance and dosing interval", "Half-life and bioavailability", "Protein binding and renal function"], "answer": "Volume of distribution and desired plasma concentration", "explanation": "LD = Vd × target Cp. Vd determines how much drug must be given to achieve a target concentration. Clearance determines the maintenance dose."},
                    {"stem": "Steady state is achieved after approximately:", "options": ["5 half-lives (97% of steady state)", "2 half-lives", "3 half-lives", "10 half-lives"], "answer": "5 half-lives (97% of steady state)", "explanation": "After 5 half-lives, 1 - (½)^5 = 96.875% of steady state is reached. This is conventionally considered steady state for clinical purposes."}
                ]
            },
            {
                "letter": "B",
                "name": "Short Answer Questions",
                "marks": 40,
                "questions": [
                    {"stem": "(a) Define bioavailability and list four factors that affect it. (4 marks)\n(b) Calculate the bioavailability of a drug if 80 mg of an oral dose reaches the systemic circulation unchanged from a 100 mg oral dose. (2 marks)\n(c) Explain why a drug with high first-pass metabolism may still be effective if given sublingually. (4 marks)", "modelAnswer": "a) Bioavailability (F) = fraction of an administered dose that reaches the systemic circulation unchanged. Factors: (1) First-pass metabolism — extraction by liver and gut; (2) Physicochemical properties — solubility, stability, pKa; (3) Formulation — dissolution rate, particle size, excipients; (4) Physiological factors — gastric pH, gastric emptying, intestinal motility, food; (5) Transporter effects — P-gp efflux.\n\nb) F = (amount reaching systemic circulation) / (administered dose) × 100% = 80/100 × 100% = 80%. F = 0.80.\n\nc) Sublingual administration: veins draining the oral mucosa empty into the superior vena cava → systemic circulation, bypassing the portal vein and liver. Therefore, first-pass metabolism is completely avoided. Nitroglycerin is a classic example — its oral F is <10%, but sublingual F approaches 100%."},
                    {"stem": "(a) Explain the clinical significance of plasma protein binding displacement interactions. (4 marks)\n(b) Describe one clinically important displacement interaction with warfarin. (3 marks)\n(c) Why is the clinical relevance of protein binding displacement often transient? (3 marks)", "modelAnswer": "a) Clinical significance: Highly bound drugs (>90%) are most affected. Displacement → increased free fraction → enhanced pharmacological effect. Most dangerous when: (1) Narrow therapeutic index drug is displaced (warfarin, phenytoin); (2) Drug has small Vd (most drug is in plasma); (3) Displacing drug also inhibits metabolism (e.g., phenylbutazone + warfarin — both displacement + metabolic inhibition).\n\nb) Warfarin + phenylbutazone/NSAIDs: (1) Displacement from albumin → increased free warfarin; (2) Phenylbutazone also inhibits warfarin metabolism (CYP2C9); (3) Combined effect → marked INR elevation → bleeding risk. Interaction can be fatal.\n\nc) Initially: displacement → increased free drug → enhanced effect. Over days: (1) Increased free drug is more available for clearance (increased CL); (2) New steady state with similar free concentration but lower total concentration; (3) The transient effect is most clinically relevant when the displaced drug has a long t½ (warfarin: takes weeks to re-equilibrate)."},
                    {"stem": "(a) Compare first-order and zero-order kinetics with respect to half-life, rate equation, and clinical examples. (5 marks)\n(b) Why does aspirin at high anti-inflammatory doses show zero-order kinetics? (2 marks)\n(c) A patient with phenytoin toxicity has a level of 35 µg/mL. How does this affect the elimination half-life compared to a level of 10 µg/mL? (3 marks)", "modelAnswer": "a) FIRST-ORDER: Rate = k·C (proportional to concentration). t½ is constant. Fraction eliminated/unit time is constant. Examples: most drugs, warfarin, digoxin, metformin.\n\nZERO-ORDER: Rate = k₀ (constant). t½ increases with dose. Amount eliminated/unit time is constant. Examples: ethanol, phenytoin (at therapeutic levels), high-dose aspirin, theophylline.\n\nb) Aspirin (salicylate) undergoes conjugation (phase II) in the liver. At low doses (<600 mg), first-order. At high anti-inflammatory doses (>3-4 g/day), the conjugation pathways become saturated → zero-order kinetics → half-life increases from 3h to 15-30h → risk of accumulation and toxicity.\n\nc) Phenytoin follows saturable (Michaelis-Menten) kinetics. At 10 µg/mL (therapeutic), t½ ~24h. At 35 µg/mL (toxic), CYP2C9 is saturated → zero-order elimination → t½ can exceed 72-100h. The higher the concentration, the longer the t½ — this is why phenytoin toxicity can be prolonged and dangerous."},
                    {"stem": "(a) List four drugs that are prodrugs and state their active metabolites. (4 marks)\n(b) Explain the clinical rationale for designing each prodrug. (4 marks)\n(c) What is a disadvantage of prodrug therapy related to pharmacogenetics? (2 marks)", "modelAnswer": "a) Prodrugs and active metabolites:\n(1) Enalapril → enalaprilat (ACE inhibitor)\n(2) Codeine → morphine (opioid analgesic)\n(3) Levodopa → dopamine (antiparkinsonian)\n(4) Dipivefrine → epinephrine (antiglaucoma)\n(5) Prednisone → prednisolone (corticosteroid)\n(6) Famciclovir → penciclovir (antiviral)\n\nb) Rationale:\n(1) Enalapril: enalaprilat is poorly absorbed orally; adding ester improves lipophilicity and oral absorption.\n(2) Codeine: codeine itself has low opioid activity; activation in the liver targets delivery.\n(3) Levodopa: dopamine cannot cross BBB; levodopa (amino acid) crosses via L-amino acid transporter, then converted.\n(4) Dipivefrine: pivalic acid esters increase corneal penetration 17× vs epinephrine.\n(5) Prednisone: prodrug (11-keto) converted to active prednisolone (11-OH) by hepatic 11β-HSD.\n\nc) Pharmacogenetic disadvantage: Codeine relies on CYP2D6 for activation. Poor metabolisers (7-10% of Caucasians) get minimal analgesia. Ultra-rapid metabolisers (1-2%) produce excessive morphine → risk of respiratory depression, death. This has led to restricted codeine use in children and contraindication in breastfeeding."},
                    {"stem": "(a) Define volume of distribution and explain its clinical significance. (3 marks)\n(b) Explain how Vd is altered in obesity, pregnancy, and neonates. (4 marks)\n(c) A patient with hypoalbuminaemia has a total phenytoin level of 7 µg/mL. Why might this be therapeutic rather than subtherapeutic? (3 marks)", "modelAnswer": "a) Vd = total amount of drug in body / plasma drug concentration. It reflects the extent of drug distribution into tissues. Clinical significance: (1) Determines loading dose (LD = Vd × Cp); (2) Indicates whether drug stays in plasma (Vd ~5L) or distributes widely (Vd >100L); (3) Helps interpret drug levels.\n\nb) (1) OBESITY: Increased adipose tissue → increased Vd for lipophilic drugs (diazepam, propofol). Dosing based on total body weight or adjusted body weight.\n(2) PREGNANCY: Plasma volume ↑50%, total body water ↑ → increased Vd for hydrophilic drugs (gentamicin). Higher doses needed.\n(3) NEONATES: TBW ~75% (adult ~60%), lower body fat, reduced protein binding → increased Vd for water-soluble drugs (aminoglycosides). Higher mg/kg doses needed.\n\nc) Phenytoin is ~90% albumin-bound. In hypoalbuminaemia, free fraction increases (from 10% to ~20-30%). A total level of 7 µg/mL may correspond to a free level of 1.4-2.1 µg/mL (therapeutic free 1-2 µg/mL). The patient may be adequately treated despite a low total level. Use corrected formula or measure free phenytoin."},
                    {"stem": "(a) Describe the acetylation polymorphism and its clinical consequences. (5 marks)\n(b) How does acetylator status affect the risk of drug-induced lupus from procainamide? (3 marks)\n(c) What monitoring is recommended for patients on drugs metabolised by acetylation? (2 marks)", "modelAnswer": "a) Acetylation polymorphism: caused by NAT2 gene variants. Slow acetylators (homozygous recessive): 50-60% Caucasians, 10-20% Asians. Rapid acetylators (heterozygous or homozygous dominant): 40-50% Caucasians, 80-90% Asians.\n\nClinical consequences for SLOW acetylators:\n(1) Isoniazid → peripheral neuropathy (pyridoxine deficiency)\n(2) Procainamide → drug-induced lupus\n(3) Sulphonamides → hypersensitivity, crystalluria\n(4) Hydralazine → drug-induced lupus\n(5) Higher drug levels at standard doses\n\nClinical consequences for RAPID acetylators:\n(1) Isoniazid → hepatotoxicity (acetylhydrazine)\n(2) May need higher doses\n\nb) Procainamide is metabolised to N-acetylprocainamide (NAPA, Class III antiarrhythmic). Slow acetylators: higher procainamide levels → earlier and more frequent drug-induced lupus (ANA positive, arthralgia, rash, fever). Rapid acetylators: produce more NAPA → less lupus from procainamide, but NAPA itself can cause lupus at high levels.\n\nc) Monitoring:\n(1) For isoniazid: LFTs monthly, assess for peripheral neuropathy, give pyridoxine 25-50 mg/day\n(2) For procainamide/hydralazine: ANA titre, symptoms of lupus (arthralgia, rash, fever), stop drug if ANA becomes positive or lupus symptoms develop\n(3) Therapeutic drug monitoring: procainamide levels (therapeutic 4-10 µg/mL) and NAPA levels (therapeutic 10-30 µg/mL)"},
                    {"stem": "(a) Explain the clinical significance of CYP450 enzyme induction, including onset/offset and examples. (4 marks)\n(b) Describe two clinically important drug interactions caused by enzyme induction. (3 marks)\n(c) A patient on warfarin starts carbamazepine. Predict the effect and management. After 3 weeks, carbamazepine is stopped. What happens to the warfarin requirement? (3 marks)", "modelAnswer": "a) CYP450 induction: increased synthesis of CYP enzymes → increased drug metabolism. Onset: 7-14 days (requires new protein synthesis). Offset: 7-14 days after stopping the inducer (enzyme levels gradually decline). Examples: rifampicin, phenytoin, carbamazepine, phenobarbitone, St John's wort, chronic ethanol.\n\nb) (1) Rifampicin + warfarin → reduced INR, risk of thrombosis; (2) Carbamazepine + oral contraceptives → contraceptive failure; (3) Phenytoin + ciclosporin → reduced ciclosporin levels, risk of transplant rejection.\n\nc) Carbamazepine induces CYP2C9 → increased warfarin metabolism → decreased INR → subtherapeutic anticoagulation. Management: increase warfarin dose (may need 2-3×), monitor INR every 2-3 days. When carbamazepine is stopped after 3 weeks: enzyme induction resolves over 7-14 days → warfarin metabolism returns to baseline → INR rises → risk of bleeding. Warfarin dose must be reduced back to original, with frequent INR monitoring during transition."},
                    {"stem": "(a) Explain the concept of therapeutic drug monitoring and list three indications for its use. (4 marks)\n(b) A 60-year-old man on lithium 800 mg daily has a random serum lithium of 1.8 mmol/L (therapeutic 0.6-1.2). He has tremor, ataxia, and confusion. Outline management. (3 marks)\n(c) What factors can alter lithium levels and require dose adjustment? (3 marks)", "modelAnswer": "a) TDM: measurement of drug concentrations to optimise therapy. Indications: (1) Narrow therapeutic index drugs (digoxin, lithium, theophylline, phenytoin, aminoglycosides); (2) Drugs with saturable kinetics (phenytoin); (3) Suspected toxicity or therapeutic failure; (4) Variable pharmacokinetics; (5) Organ impairment affecting clearance.\n\nb) This is lithium toxicity (severe). Management:\n(1) STOP lithium immediately\n(2) Check: serum lithium, electrolytes (K+, Na+), renal function, thyroid function\n(3) IV 0.9% NaCl fluids (lithium is handled like Na+ in the kidney — Na+ repletion promotes Li+ excretion)\n(4) Severe toxicity (level >2.5 mmol/L, or 1.8 with symptoms): consider haemodialysis (lithium is small, non-protein-bound, dialysable)\n(5) Monitor level q6-12h, ECG, neurological status\n(6) Avoid: NSAIDs, thiazides (increase lithium levels)\n\nc) Factors increasing lithium levels (need dose reduction):\n(1) Dehydration, Na+ depletion (diuretics, vomiting, diarrhoea)\n(2) Renal impairment (reduced clearance)\n(3) Drug interactions: NSAIDs (reduce GFR), thiazides (increase Li+ reabsorption), ACE-I/ARBs (reduce GFR)\n(4) Fever, sweating\nFactors decreasing lithium levels (may need dose increase):\n(1) High Na+ intake\n(2) Caffeine (increases Li+ excretion)"}
                ]
            },
            {
                "letter": "C",
                "name": "Long Answer Questions",
                "marks": 30,
                "questions": [
                    {"stem": "A 72-year-old woman (80 kg) with atrial fibrillation, hypertension, and heart failure with reduced ejection fraction is on digoxin 0.25 mg daily, warfarin, lisinopril, and furosemide. She presents to the emergency department with nausea, vomiting, blurred vision (seeing yellow-green halos), and palpitations. Her heart rate is 50 bpm and irregular. ECG shows atrial fibrillation with slow ventricular response and multiple PVCs. Digoxin level is 3.8 ng/mL. Serum K+ is 3.1 mmol/L, Mg2+ is 1.6 mg/dL, creatinine is 1.8 mg/dL (eGFR 32 mL/min).\n\na) Analyse all the factors that contributed to digoxin toxicity in this patient. Include pharmacokinetic, pharmacodynamic, and drug interaction factors. (8 marks)\nb) Explain the mechanism by which hypokalaemia potentiates digoxin toxicity at the molecular level. (6 marks)\nc) Describe the step-by-step management of this patient's digoxin toxicity, including the indications, dosing, and administration of digoxin-specific Fab antibody fragments. (8 marks)\nd) After recovery, what would be an appropriate digoxin dosing strategy? Discuss alternative rate-control options for this patient given her HFrEF and renal impairment. (8 marks)", "modelAnswer": "a) Contributing factors:\n\nPHARMACOKINETIC:\n(1) RENAL IMPAIRMENT (eGFR 32): Digoxin is 60-80% renally excreted unchanged. With eGFR 32, CL is markedly reduced → t½ prolonged from 36h to 80-120h → accumulation with standard dose.\n(2) REDUCED Vd: In CKD, tissue binding of digoxin (to Na/K-ATPase in muscle) is reduced → Vd decreases → higher plasma concentration for the same dose.\n(3) HYPOKALAEMIA (3.1): Reduces competition for digoxin binding to Na/K-ATPase → enhanced toxicity.\n(4) HYPOMAGNESAEMIA (1.6): Mg2+ deficiency predisposes to digoxin toxicity and arrhythmias.\n\nPHARMACODYNAMIC:\n(1) HFrEF: compromised myocardium more susceptible to digoxin toxicity.\n(2) Advanced age: reduced renal function, reduced Vd.\n\nDRUG INTERACTIONS:\n(1) FUROSEMIDE: causes K+ and Mg2+ loss → hypokalaemia → potentiates digoxin toxicity.\n(2) LISINOPRIL: can reduce GFR → further reduces digoxin clearance.\n\nb) Molecular mechanism:\n\nDigoxin binds to the α-subunit of Na+/K+-ATPase on cardiac myocytes, inhibiting the pump. K+ and digoxin compete for the same extracellular binding site on Na/K-ATPase.\n\nIn NORMOKALAEMIA: K+ competes effectively → limits digoxin binding → some pump function preserved.\n\nIn HYPOKALAEMIA (3.1 mmol/L): Reduced extracellular K+ → less competition → MORE digoxin binds to Na/K-ATPase → greater pump inhibition.\n\nConsequences:\n(1) Intracellular Na+ accumulation → reduced Na+/Ca2+ exchanger activity → intracellular Ca2+ overload\n(2) Ca2+ overload → delayed after-depolarisations (DADs) → triggered arrhythmias (PVCs, atrial tachycardia with block, VT/VF)\n(3) Reduced resting membrane potential (less negative) → increased automaticity\n(4) Decreased conduction velocity → AV block, bradycardia\n\nc) Management:\n\nSTEP 1: ABC and MONITORING\n- Continuous ECG monitoring, IV access, oxygen\n- STOP digoxin and furosemide\n- Check: digoxin level, K+, Mg2+, Ca2+, Cr, BUN, ECG\n\nSTEP 2: CORRECT ELECTROLYTES\n- K+: target 4.5-5.0 mmol/L. Give IV KCl 20-40 mmol over 2-4h (max 10 mmol/h). Monitor ECG and K+.\n- Mg2+: give MgSO4 2 g IV over 15-30 min if Mg <2.0 mg/dL. Mg2+ stabilises myocardium and suppresses DADs.\n\nSTEP 3: DIGOXIN-SPECIFIC Fab (Digibind/DigiFab)\n\nINDICATIONS (this patient meets multiple):\n- Life-threatening arrhythmias (PVCs, slow AF, AV block)\n- Digoxin level >2.0 ng/mL + symptoms\n- Haemodynamic instability (if present)\n- Severe toxicity with hyperkalaemia >5.5 (not yet but may develop)\n\nDOSING:\nMethod (serum level): vials = (level ng/mL × weight kg) / 100\n= (3.8 × 80) / 100 = 3.04 → 3 vials (120 mg)\nAlternative: 3-6 vials empirically for moderate-severe toxicity\n\nADMINISTRATION: IV over 30 minutes through 0.22 micron filter. Onset 30-60 min.\n\nAFTER Fab:\n- Free digoxin level becomes undetectable (but total digoxin level rises due to Fab-bound complex — do not misinterpret this as worsening)\n- Monitor: ECG, K+, Mg2+, digoxin levels clinical response\n- May need repeat dose if no response after 1-2h\n\nSTEP 4: BRADYCARDIA / ARRHYTHMIA MANAGEMENT\n- Atropine 0.5-1 mg IV PRN for bradycardia\n- Temporary pacing if symptomatic bradycardia/AV block unresponsive\n- Avoid: isoprenaline (proarrhythmic in digoxin-toxic myocardium)\n- Avoid: DC cardioversion unless refractory VT/VF (risk of asystole)\n\nSTEP 5: MONITORING\n- Continuous ECG for ≥24h post-resolution\n- K+, Mg2+, Cr q6-12h\n- Monitor for rebound hypokalaemia after Fab\n- Cautious volume repletion (HFrEF — avoid fluid overload)\n\nd) Future dosing:\n\nDIGOXIN:\n- For eGFR 32, appropriate dose: 0.0625 mg daily or 0.125 mg every other day\n- Target trough level: 0.5-1.0 ng/mL (lower end for HF)\n- Start after electrolytes and renal function stabilised\n- Monitor trough levels after 5-7 days of therapy (5 t½ for new SS)\n\nALTERNATIVE RATE CONTROL OPTIONS:\n\n(1) BISOPROLOL: beta1-selective, reduces HR, improves mortality in HFrEF (CIBIS-II). Start 1.25 mg daily, titrate slowly. Renally cleared (caution).\n(2) CARVEDILOL: combined alpha/beta-blocker, hepatic metabolism (safer in CKD). Start 3.125 mg BID, titrate q2 weeks. Proven mortality benefit in HF (COPERNICUS).\n(3) METOPROLOL SUCCINATE: beta1-selective, once daily. MERIT-HF proven.\n- Beta-blockers are preferred: they provide mortality benefit in HFrEF, which digoxin does not.\n\n(4) IVABRADINE: If beta-blockers contraindicated or insufficient. Reduces HR via If channel blockade. No negative inotropy. Excreted via CYP3A4 (not renal).\n\n(5) DILTIAZEM: Use cautiously — negative inotrope. Only if beta-blockers cannot be used and AF is problematic. Avoid in decompensated HF.\n\nAVOID: amiodarone (increases digoxin 2×, pulmonary/thyroid toxicity), verapamil (negative inotrope, ↑ digoxin levels), sotalol (proarrhythmic in CKD).\n\nRECOMMENDATION: Start bisoprolol 1.25 mg daily. If insufficient rate control, add low-dose digoxin 0.0625 mg daily. Monitor HR, BP, LV function, electrolytes, digoxin levels."},
                    {"stem": "A 45-year-old woman (60 kg) with new-onset focal seizures is started on phenytoin 300 mg daily. She has a history of hypothyroidism (on levothyroxine 100 µg daily) and depression (on sertraline 50 mg daily). Her serum albumin is 2.5 g/dL (low). After 10 days on phenytoin, her total phenytoin level is 6.5 µg/mL. She has had no further seizures. She also reports increased fatigue and her TSH is now 8.5 mIU/L (previously 2.1 on levothyroxine).\n\na) Explain why the total phenytoin level of 6.5 µg/mL may be therapeutic in this patient. Show the corrected calculation. (6 marks)\nb) Describe the saturable (Michaelis-Menten) pharmacokinetics of phenytoin. If the dose is increased from 300 mg to 330 mg daily, why might the concentration rise disproportionately? (6 marks)\nc) Analyse the drug interaction between phenytoin and levothyroxine. Explain the mechanism and how you would manage this patient's now-elevated TSH. (6 marks)\nd) Discuss the interaction between phenytoin and sertraline. What would you monitor? (4 marks)\ne) If this patient needs an alternative anticonvulsant due to the drug interactions, which agent(s) would you recommend? Justify your choice based on her other medications and conditions. (8 marks)", "modelAnswer": "a) Phenytoin is ~90% albumin-bound. With albumin 2.5 g/dL (normal 3.5-5.0), free fraction increases from ~10% to ~15-20%.\n\nSheiner-Tozer correction for hypoalbuminaemia:\nCorrected total = measured total / (0.9 × albumin + 0.1)\n= 6.5 / (0.9 × 2.5 + 0.1)\n= 6.5 / (2.25 + 0.1)\n= 6.5 / 2.35\n= 2.77 µg/mL\n\nThis correction suggests the level is low. HOWEVER, this formula is imperfect — it was designed for nephrotic syndrome and may underestimate in simple hypoalbuminaemia.\n\nA better interpretation: free phenytoin = 6.5 × 0.15 (assuming 15% free) = 0.975 µg/mL. If free fraction is 20%: free = 1.3 µg/mL.\n\nTarget free phenytoin = 1-2 µg/mL. So her free level is likely at the lower end of therapeutic. This EXPLAINS why she is seizure-free despite a low total level.\n\nBEST PRACTICE: Measure FREE phenytoin directly. But clinically, the fact she is seizure-free without toxicity suggests adequate therapy.\n\nb) Phenytoin follows Michaelis-Menten kinetics:\nRate of metabolism = Vmax × C / (Km + C)\n\nWhere:\n- Vmax = maximum metabolic rate (typically 400-500 mg/day)\n- Km = concentration at half Vmax (typically 4-8 µg/mL)\n\nAt low concentrations (C << Km): rate ∝ C (first-order). At high concentrations (C >> Km): rate = Vmax (zero-order).\n\nAt therapeutic concentrations, CYP2C9 is near saturation. Small dose increases produce disproportionate concentration rises.\n\nExample: if Vmax = 500, Km = 5, at 300 mg/day:\n300 = 500 × C / (5 + C)\n300(5 + C) = 500C\n1500 + 300C = 500C\n1500 = 200C\nC = 7.5 µg/mL\n\nAt 330 mg/day:\n330 = 500 × C / (5 + C)\n330(5 + C) = 500C\n1650 + 330C = 500C\n1650 = 170C\nC = 9.7 µg/mL\n\nSo 300 → 330 (10% increase) → Cp from 7.5 → 9.7 (29% increase). If she is more saturated (e.g., Vmax lower or higher starting Cp), the increase could be even larger. Dose adjustments should be small (25-50 mg) with TDM.\n\nc) Phenytoin ↔ levothyroxine interaction:\n\nMECHANISM: Phenytoin is a potent inducer of CYP3A4 and UGT glucuronosyltransferases. It increases the hepatic metabolism of thyroid hormones (T4 and T3) → increased clearance → reduced serum thyroxine levels. Phenytoin also displaces T4 from thyroid-binding globulin (TBG).\n\nAdditionally, phenytoin itself suppresses TSH secretion (central effect).\n\nMANAGEMENT:\n(1) Confirm the aetiology: this is NOT primary hypothyroidism worsening — it's a drug-induced increase in levothyroxine requirement.\n(2) Increase levothyroxine dose: typically need to increase by 25-50%. Start levothyroxine 125 µg daily (increase from 100 µg).\n(3) Recheck TSH in 6-8 weeks.\n(4) If phenytoin is ever discontinued: levothyroxine dose must be reduced (enzyme induction resolves → thyroid hormone levels rise → risk of thyrotoxicosis).\n\nAlternative: consider switching phenytoin to a non-inducing anticonvulsant (see part e).\n\nd) Phenytoin ↔ sertraline interaction:\n\nPHENYTOIN AS VICTIM: Sertraline is a mild inhibitor of CYP2C9 and CYP2C19. It may INCREASE phenytoin levels → risk of phenytoin toxicity. However, sertraline is a relatively weak inhibitor — the effect is modest.\n\nPHENYTOIN AS PERPETRATOR: Phenytoin induces CYP3A4 and CYP2C19 → INCREASES sertraline metabolism → DECREASED sertraline levels → potential loss of antidepressant efficacy.\n\nMONITORING:\n(1) Phenytoin: free and total levels, signs of toxicity (nystagmus, ataxia, sedation)\n(2) Sertraline: mood, depressive symptoms\n(3) If depression worsens: consider sertraline dose increase or switch to antidepressant not metabolised by CYP3A4/CYP2C19 (e.g., citalopram — less CYP interaction)\n\ne) Alternative anticonvulsant recommendations:\n\nGiven her drug interactions (phenytoin affecting levothyroxine and sertraline), an anticonvulsant with minimal enzyme induction is preferred.\n\nBEST CHOICE: LEVETIRACETAM\n- Mechanism: SV2A binding, modulates neurotransmitter release\n- Protein binding: <10% (no albumin issue, no displacement)\n- Kinetics: linear, predictable\n- Metabolism: minimal hepatic (66% renally excreted unchanged)\n- Drug interactions: NONE clinically significant — does not induce or inhibit CYP450, does not affect thyroid hormone metabolism\n- Dose: 500-1000 mg BID (adjust for renal function; with normal Cr, standard dosing)\n- Monitoring: renal function, behaviour (rare psychiatric effects)\n- Advantage: will not interact with levothyroxine or sertraline\n\nGOOD ALTERNATIVE: LAMOTRIGINE\n- Mechanism: Na+ channel blockade, glutamate inhibition\n- Protein binding: ~55% (moderate)\n- Kinetics: linear\n- Drug interactions: minimal CYP interactions; valproate inhibits (not relevant here), enzyme inducers increase clearance\n- Caution: slow titration (risk of SJS)\n- Dose: 25-400 mg daily (titrate over 6-8 weeks)\n- Not ideal for acute initiation (slow titration required)\n\nACCEPTABLE ALTERNATIVE: GABAPENTIN/PREGABALIN (if focal seizures)\n- No CYP interactions\n- Renal excretion\n- But: less efficacious for focal seizures than levetiracetam/lamotrigine\n\nAVOID:\n- Carbamazepine: potent CYP inducer (affects levothyroxine, sertraline)\n- Phenobarbitone: CYP inducer\n- Valproate: highly protein-bound, displaces levothyroxine from TBG, inhibits lamotrigine metabolism\n\nRECOMMENDATION: Transition from phenytoin to levetiracetam 500 mg BID. Start levetiracetam, then taper phenytoin over 2-4 weeks. Monitor: seizure frequency, TSH (may need to reduce levothyroxine dose as phenytoin induction resolves), mood (sertraline levels will rise as phenytoin induction resolves — monitor for SSRI side effects)."}
                ]
            }
        ]
