                "questions": [
                    {"stem": "Which of the following drugs is a prodrug that requires activation by ester hydrolysis?", "options": ["Enalapril", "Captopril", "Lisinopril", "Losartan"], "answer": "Enalapril", "explanation": "Enalapril is a prodrug hydrolysed by hepatic esterases to enalaprilat. Captopril and lisinopril are active without metabolism."},
                    {"stem": "The primary advantage of dipivefrine over epinephrine in ophthalmology is:", "options": ["Enhanced corneal penetration due to increased lipophilicity", "Greater aqueous solubility", "Longer half-life in the eye", "Reduced systemic absorption"], "answer": "Enhanced corneal penetration due to increased lipophilicity", "explanation": "Dipivefrine is a lipophilic diester prodrug that penetrates the cornea 17 times better than epinephrine."},
                    {"stem": "Mercaptopurine toxicity is potentiated by allopurinol because allopurinol:", "options": ["Inhibits xanthine oxidase, reducing mercaptopurine metabolism", "Inhibits HGPRT, reducing mercaptopurine activation", "Displaces mercaptopurine from plasma proteins", "Induces mercaptopurine absorption"], "answer": "Inhibits xanthine oxidase, reducing mercaptopurine metabolism", "explanation": "Allopurinol inhibits xanthine oxidase, the enzyme that metabolites mercaptopurine. Mercaptopurine dose must be reduced by 75%."},
                    {"stem": "Ketoconazole inhibits CYP3A4. Which of the following drugs would have increased levels when co-administered?", "options": ["Ciclosporin", "Penicillin", "Metformin", "Methotrexate"], "answer": "Ciclosporin", "explanation": "Ciclosporin is a CYP3A4 substrate. Ketoconazole inhibits its metabolism → increased ciclosporin levels → nephrotoxicity risk."},
                    {"stem": "Cimetidine is a CYP450 inhibitor, unlike other H2RAs, because:", "options": ["Its imidazole ring binds to the haem iron of CYP450", "It has longer duration of action", "It is more potent at H2 receptors", "It undergoes extensive hepatic metabolism"], "answer": "Its imidazole ring binds to the haem iron of CYP450", "explanation": "Cimetidine's imidazole ring coordinates with the haem iron of CYP450, inhibiting multiple isoforms (1A2, 2C9, 2D6, 3A4). Ranitidine and famotidine lack this structure."},
                    {"stem": "Rifampicin causes failure of hormonal contraceptives. The best alternative contraceptive method is:", "options": ["Non-hormonal methods (barrier, copper IUD)", "Higher-dose oral contraceptive pill", "Progestin-only implant", "Vaginal contraceptive ring"], "answer": "Non-hormonal methods (barrier, copper IUD)", "explanation": "Rifampicin induces CYP3A4, increasing metabolism of all hormonal contraceptives. Non-hormonal methods are recommended."},
                    {"stem": "Carbamazepine induces its own metabolism. This phenomenon is called:", "options": ["Autoinduction", "First-pass effect", "Enterohepatic recirculation", "Countertransport"], "answer": "Autoinduction", "explanation": "Carbamazepine induces CYP3A4, accelerating its own metabolism. This causes its half-life to decrease from ~36h (first dose) to ~8-12h (after 3-5 weeks)."},
                    {"stem": "Volume of distribution of digoxin is approximately 500 L because:", "options": ["It extensively binds to Na/K-ATPase in skeletal muscle and other tissues", "It is highly protein-bound and confined to plasma", "It is sequestered in adipose tissue", "It is actively transported into erythrocytes"], "answer": "It extensively binds to Na/K-ATPase in skeletal muscle and other tissues", "explanation": "Digoxin binds to Na/K-ATPase in muscle, heart, and other tissues → extensive tissue distribution → large Vd (~7 L/kg)."},
                    {"stem": "In obese patients, the Vd of propofol is increased because:", "options": ["Propofol is lipophilic and distributes into adipose tissue", "Obese patients have increased total body water", "Obesity increases albumin binding", "Propofol is sequestered in the liver"], "answer": "Propofol is lipophilic and distributes into adipose tissue", "explanation": "Propofol is highly lipophilic (logP ~3.8). Obese patients have more adipose tissue → increased Vd → slower elimination."},
                    {"stem": "A drug with high plasma protein binding is more likely to:", "options": ["Be displaced by other highly bound drugs, increasing free fraction", "Have a large volume of distribution", "Be rapidly cleared by the kidneys", "Have a rapid onset of action"], "answer": "Be displaced by other highly bound drugs, increasing free fraction", "explanation": "Displacement from albumin increases free drug. This is clinically significant for narrow-TI drugs like warfarin and phenytoin."},
                    {"stem": "The rapid recovery from thiopentone anaesthesia is primarily due to:", "options": ["Redistribution to skeletal muscle and adipose tissue", "Rapid hepatic metabolism", "Renal excretion", "Biliary elimination"], "answer": "Redistribution to skeletal muscle and adipose tissue", "explanation": "Thiopentone is highly lipid-soluble. It rapidly enters the brain (highly perfused), then redistributes to less vascular tissues, terminating its anaesthetic effect."},
                    {"stem": "Slow acetylators of procainamide have a higher risk of:", "options": ["Drug-induced lupus erythematosus", "Peripheral neuropathy", "Hepatotoxicity", "Cardiac arrhythmias"], "answer": "Drug-induced lupus erythematosus", "explanation": "Slow acetylators accumulate procainamide, which can trigger drug-induced lupus (arthralgia, rash, ANA positive). Occurs in ~30% of slow acetylators on long-term therapy."},
                    {"stem": "Rapid acetylators of hydralazine are at lower risk of:", "options": ["Drug-induced lupus", "Peripheral neuropathy", "Hepatotoxicity", "Hypotension"], "answer": "Drug-induced lupus", "explanation": "Hydralazine is metabolised by NAT2. Slow acetylators accumulate hydralazine → higher risk of drug-induced lupus. Rapid acetylators have lower risk."},
                    {"stem": "When is a loading dose most clinically important?", "options": ["When rapid therapeutic effect is needed and the drug has a long half-life", "When the drug has a short half-life", "When renal function is impaired", "When the drug is highly protein-bound"], "answer": "When rapid therapeutic effect is needed and the drug has a long half-life", "explanation": "Loading dose rapidly achieves therapeutic concentration without waiting 5 half-lives. Essential for drugs like amiodarone (t½ ~58 days), digoxin (t½ ~36h), and phenytoin."},
                    {"stem": "A patient on gentamicin develops nephrotoxicity. Monitoring gentamicin levels is important because:", "options": ["It has a narrow therapeutic index with risk of nephrotoxicity and ototoxicity", "It is highly protein-bound", "It undergoes extensive hepatic metabolism", "It has very high oral bioavailability"], "answer": "It has a narrow therapeutic index with risk of nephrotoxicity and ototoxicity", "explanation": "Aminoglycosides (gentamicin, tobramycin) have narrow TI. Trough levels >2 µg/mL increase nephrotoxicity risk. Peak levels guide efficacy."},
                    {"stem": "After 3 half-lives of a drug following first-order kinetics, the percentage eliminated is:", "options": ["87.5%", "50%", "75%", "93.75%"], "answer": "87.5%", "explanation": "After 3 half-lives: fraction eliminated = 1 - (½)^3 = 1 - 1/8 = 0.875 = 87.5%."},
                    {"stem": "The bioavailability of an orally administered drug is MOST reduced by:", "options": ["Extensive first-pass hepatic metabolism", "Rapid gastric emptying", "High-fat meal", "Concurrent antacid use"], "answer": "Extensive first-pass hepatic metabolism", "explanation": "First-pass metabolism reduces the fraction of drug reaching systemic circulation. Extraction ratios >0.7 mean <30% bioavailability."},
                    {"stem": "Grapefruit juice interacts with which of the following drug classes most significantly?", "options": ["Statins (simvastatin, atorvastatin, lovastatin)", "Penicillins", "Beta-blockers", "Diuretics"], "answer": "Statins (simvastatin, atorvastatin, lovastatin)", "explanation": "These statins are CYP3A4 substrates. Grapefruit juice increases their levels → myopathy/rhabdomyolysis risk. Pravastatin and rosuvastatin are safe."},
                    {"stem": "A weakly acidic drug (pKa 3.5) will be mostly ionised at blood pH 7.4. Its absorption in the stomach (pH 1.5) will be:", "options": ["Rapid because it is mostly non-ionised at low pH", "Slow because it is mostly ionised at low pH", "Unaffected by pH because absorption is active", "Complete only in the presence of food"], "answer": "Rapid because it is mostly non-ionised at low pH", "explanation": "At pH 1.5 (<< pKa 3.5), the weak acid is predominantly non-ionised (HA form) → lipid-soluble → rapid passive diffusion."},
                    {"stem": "Weak bases (e.g., morphine, pKa 8.0) are absorbed poorly in the stomach because:", "options": ["At acidic pH, they are mostly ionised and cannot cross lipid membranes", "At acidic pH, they are mostly non-ionised and rapidly metabolised", "Gastric mucosa lacks transporters for weak bases", "Gastric acid degrades weak bases"], "answer": "At acidic pH, they are mostly ionised and cannot cross lipid membranes", "explanation": "For a weak base with pKa 8.0 at pH 1.5: [BH+]/[B] = 10^(pKa-pH) = 10^6.5 = ~3 million:1 → almost entirely ionised → poor absorption."},
                    {"stem": "The half-life of a drug is independent of dose in:", "options": ["First-order kinetics", "Zero-order kinetics", "Michaelis-Menten kinetics at saturation", "All kinetic models"], "answer": "First-order kinetics", "explanation": "In first-order kinetics, a constant fraction is eliminated per unit time → t½ is constant. In zero-order kinetics, t½ increases with dose."},
                    {"stem": "For a drug that is 90% renally excreted unchanged, severe renal impairment (eGFR 15) will:", "options": ["Increase the half-life approximately 6-fold", "Decrease the half-life", "Have no effect on pharmacokinetics", "Increase the volume of distribution"], "answer": "Increase the half-life approximately 6-fold", "explanation": "If CL is reduced to 1/6 (from eGFR 90 to 15), and Vd unchanged: t½ = 0.693 × Vd / CL → t½ increases 6-fold."},
                    {"stem": "Drugs undergoing enterohepatic recirculation include:", "options": ["Oral contraceptives (ethinyloestradiol)", "Aminoglycosides", "Penicillins", "Lithium"], "answer": "Oral contraceptives (ethinyloestradiol)", "explanation": "Ethinyloestradiol undergoes enterohepatic recirculation. Antibiotics that reduce gut flora can interrupt this → reduced contraceptive efficacy."},
                    {"stem": "Which of the following is a CYP450 inhibitor used clinically for its interaction profile?", "options": ["Ritonavir", "Rifampicin", "Carbamazepine", "Phenobarbitone"], "answer": "Ritonavir", "explanation": "Ritonavir is a potent CYP3A4 inhibitor, used pharmacokinetically to boost levels of other HIV protease inhibitors."},
                    {"stem": "Free drug concentration is most affected by:", "options": ["Changes in protein binding (hypoalbuminaemia, displacement)", "Changes in hepatic blood flow", "Changes in renal function", "Changes in gastric pH"], "answer": "Changes in protein binding (hypoalbuminaemia, displacement)", "explanation": "For highly bound drugs, small changes in binding cause large changes in free fraction. Total levels may be misleading in hypoalbuminaemia."},
                    {"stem": "The dose of theophylline must be reduced when co-prescribed with ciprofloxacin because:", "options": ["Ciprofloxacin inhibits CYP1A2, reducing theophylline metabolism", "Ciprofloxacin induces theophylline clearance", "Both drugs are nephrotoxic", "Ciprofloxacin displaces theophylline from albumin"], "answer": "Ciprofloxacin inhibits CYP1A2, reducing theophylline metabolism", "explanation": "Ciprofloxacin is a CYP1A2 inhibitor. Theophylline is primarily metabolised by CYP1A2. Inhibition → theophylline accumulation → toxicity."},
                    {"stem": "The elimination rate for a zero-order drug is:", "options": ["Constant regardless of concentration", "Proportional to concentration", "Inversely proportional to dose", "Exponentially declining"], "answer": "Constant regardless of concentration", "explanation": "Zero-order: rate = k₀ (constant amount per time). Examples: ethanol, phenytoin at saturation, aspirin at high doses."},
                    {"stem": "The ideal drug for therapeutic drug monitoring has:", "options": ["Narrow therapeutic index, variable kinetics, and good correlation between concentration and effect", "Wide therapeutic index and linear kinetics", "Rapid onset and short half-life", "No active metabolites"], "answer": "Narrow therapeutic index, variable kinetics, and good correlation between concentration and effect", "explanation": "TDM is useful when: (1) narrow TI; (2) significant PK variability; (3) good Cp-effect correlation; (4) difficult to assess clinical endpoint."},
                    {"stem": "Lithium monitoring should include which parameters?", "options": ["Serum lithium, thyroid function (TSH), renal function (Cr, eGFR), electrolytes", "Lithium level only", "Lithium level and FBC", "Lithium level and LFTs"], "answer": "Serum lithium, thyroid function (TSH), renal function (Cr, eGFR), electrolytes", "explanation": "Lithium causes: nephrogenic diabetes insipidus (monitor Cr, eGFR, electrolytes), hypothyroidism (monitor TSH), and toxicity (monitor Li+ level q3-6 months)."}
                ]
            },
            {
                "letter": "B",
                "name": "Short Answer Questions",
                "marks": 40,
                "questions": [
                    {"stem": "(a) Describe the role of cytochrome P450 enzymes in drug metabolism. (3 marks)\n(b) Explain why CYP450 inhibition is rapid in onset while induction takes days to weeks. (3 marks)\n(c) Give two clinical examples of CYP450 inhibition interactions and two of CYP450 induction interactions. (4 marks)", "modelAnswer": "a) CYP450 enzymes are a superfamily of haem-containing monooxygenases responsible for Phase I drug metabolism. They catalyse: (1) Oxidation (most common) — hydroxylation, N/O-dealkylation; (2) Reduction; (3) Hydrolysis. Major isoforms: CYP3A4 (metabolises ~50% of drugs), CYP2D6, CYP2C9, CYP2C19, CYP1A2. They introduce or expose functional groups, making drugs more polar and preparing them for Phase II conjugation.\n\nb) INHIBITION: Direct binding to the CYP enzyme (competitive or mechanism-based). As soon as the inhibitor reaches sufficient intracellular concentration, enzyme activity is reduced → rapid onset (hours).\n\nINDUCTION: Requires transcription factor activation (e.g., PXR, CAR) → increased gene transcription → mRNA synthesis → new CYP protein synthesis. This takes days to weeks. Offset also takes days (enzymes degrade and are not replaced).\n\nc) INHIBITION interactions:\n(1) Ketoconazole + terfenadine → QT prolongation (fatal arrhythmias)\n(2) Ciprofloxacin + theophylline → theophylline toxicity\n(3) Amiodarone + warfarin → elevated INR\n\nINDUCTION interactions:\n(1) Rifampicin + oral contraceptives → contraceptive failure\n(2) Phenytoin + warfarin → decreased INR\n(3) Carbamazepine + ciclosporin → transplant rejection risk"},
                    {"stem": "(a) Explain the difference between competitive and non-competitive enzyme inhibition. (3 marks)\n(b) How does the 'grapefruit juice effect' differ from most drug-drug CYP interactions? (3 marks)\n(c) Name four drug classes that are affected by grapefruit juice. (4 marks)", "modelAnswer": "a) COMPETITIVE: Inhibitor competes with substrate for the active site → reversible by increasing substrate concentration. Example: cimetidine, fluconazole.\nNON-COMPETITIVE: Binds to a site other than the active site → cannot be overcome by substrate. IRREVERSIBLE (mechanism-based): Inhibitor is metabolised to a reactive intermediate that covalently binds to the enzyme → permanent inactivation → requires new enzyme synthesis. Example: grapefruit juice, erythromycin, ketoconazole (partially).\n\nb) Grapefruit juice effect is UNIQUE because:\n- Mechanism: irreversible (mechanism-based) inhibition of intestinal CYP3A4 ONLY (not hepatic)\n- Effect: reduces pre-systemic metabolism in gut wall → increases oral bioavailability of substrates\n- No effect on IV drug levels\n- Duration: 24-72 hours (requires new CYP3A4 synthesis in enterocytes)\n- Single glass can have significant effect\n\nc) Drug classes affected by grapefruit juice:\n(1) STATINS: simvastatin, atorvastatin, lovastatin (↑ myopathy risk)\n(2) CALCIUM CHANNEL BLOCKERS: felodipine, nifedipine, amlodipine (↑ hypotension)\n(3) IMMUNOSUPPRESSANTS: ciclosporin, tacrolimus (↑ nephrotoxicity)\n(4) BENZODIAZEPINES: midazolam, triazolam (↑ sedation)\n(5) ANTIHISTAMINES: terfenadine, astemizole (↑ QT prolongation)\n(6) ANTIVIRALS: saquinavir, maraviroc"},
                    {"stem": "(a) What is the loading dose? Derive the equation LD = Vd × Cp. (3 marks)\n(b) A 70 kg patient needs a theophylline loading dose. Vd = 0.5 L/kg, desired Cp = 15 mg/L, F = 1. Calculate the LD. (2 marks)\n(c) Why is the loading dose of digoxin affected by renal function, even though it is given as a single dose? (2 marks)\n(d) A patient has been on a maintenance dose of a drug with t½ = 24h for 3 days. Has steady state been reached? Explain. (3 marks)", "modelAnswer": "a) Loading dose: a larger initial dose given to rapidly achieve the target plasma concentration.\n\nDerivation: At time 0 (after distribution), amount of drug in body = Vd × Cp. The loading dose must equal this amount (minus any fraction lost during absorption). Therefore LD = Vd × Cp_target / F.\n\nb) LD = Vd × Cp / F = (0.5 L/kg × 70 kg) × 15 mg/L / 1 = 35 L × 15 mg/L = 525 mg. Clinical dose: 500-600 mg.\n\nc) Digoxin's Vd depends on tissue binding (Na/K-ATPase). In renal impairment, tissue binding is reduced → Vd decreases → for the same LD, the achieved Cp is HIGHER. So LD may need to be reduced in severe CKD despite digoxin being mostly renally cleared for maintenance.\n\nd) t½ = 24h. After 3 days (3 half-lives): fraction of SS = 1 - (½)^3 = 1 - 1/8 = 87.5% of SS. The conventional definition of SS is after 5 half-lives (97%). After 3 days, steady state is NOT reached. It takes 5 days (5 × 24h)."},
                    {"stem": "(a) Explain the concept of 'aging' in the context of organophosphate-AChE binding and its clinical significance for pralidoxime therapy. (4 marks)\n(b) How does the rate of aging differ between different organophosphates? (2 marks)\n(c) Why is pralidoxime more effective at nicotinic than muscarinic synapses? (2 marks)\n(d) What is the role of pralidoxime in treating carbamate poisoning? (2 marks)", "modelAnswer": "a) Aging: the OP-AChE complex undergoes dealkylation (loss of an alkyl group from the OP molecule), forming a very stable, irreversible complex. After aging, the AChE cannot be reactivated by pralidoxime. Clinical significance: pralidoxime must be given BEFORE aging occurs (typically within 24-48h for most OPs). Once aged, only new AChE synthesis (weeks) can restore enzyme function.\n\nb) Aging rate varies widely:\n- Soman (GD): ~2 minutes (extremely fast → pralidoxime often ineffective)\n- Sarin (GB): ~5 hours\n- VX: ~48 hours\n- Malathion, parathion: ~24-48 hours\n- Tabun (GA): ~14 hours\n\nRapid-aging agents require immediate pralidoxime and may need higher doses.\n\nc) Pralidoxime is a quaternary ammonium compound → does NOT readily cross cell membranes. At muscarinic synapses (CNS, autonomic ganglia), AChE is intracellular or within synaptic clefts that are less accessible. At the NMJ (nicotinic), the synaptic cleft is wider and AChE is more accessible to pralidoxime → better reactivation → improved muscle strength, reduced fasciculations.\n\nThis is why atropine (not pralidoxime) is the primary agent for reversing muscarinic symptoms.\n\nd) Carbamates (e.g., neostigmine, physostigmine, carbaryl) also inhibit AChE, but the carbamate-AChE complex is spontaneously reversible within hours (decarbamylation). Pralidoxime is NOT needed for carbamate poisoning and may worsen toxicity. Atropine alone is sufficient for carbamate poisoning."},
                    {"stem": "(a) Define first-order kinetics and write the equation for elimination half-life. (3 marks)\n(b) Drug X has a half-life of 6 hours. A patient receives 500 mg IV. How much drug remains after 18 hours? Show your calculation. (3 marks)\n(c) If the same drug follows zero-order kinetics with an elimination rate of 20 mg/h, how much remains after 18 hours? (2 marks)\n(d) What clinical significance does the difference between first-order and zero-order have for dosing? (2 marks)", "modelAnswer": "a) First-order kinetics: Rate of elimination is proportional to drug concentration. A CONSTANT FRACTION of drug is eliminated per unit time. dC/dt = -k·C. Half-life equation: t½ = 0.693 / k, where k = elimination rate constant. Also: t½ = 0.693 × Vd / CL.\n\nb) Number of half-lives elapsed = 18/6 = 3. Fraction remaining = (½)^n = (½)^3 = 1/8 = 0.125. Amount remaining = 500 × 0.125 = 62.5 mg.\n\nc) Zero-order: constant amount eliminated per unit time. Amount eliminated = rate × time = 20 mg/h × 18h = 360 mg. Amount remaining = 500 - 360 = 140 mg.\n\nd) Clinical significance:\n- First-order: t½ constant, predictable accumulation, steady state reached after 5 t½, dose adjustments produce proportional Cp changes.\n- Zero-order: t½ increases with dose (saturable), small dose increases can cause large Cp increases (phenytoin, high-dose aspirin), risk of accumulation and toxicity. TDM essential. Loading and maintenance dose calculations differ (Michaelis-Menten)."},
                    {"stem": "(a) Describe the phenomenon of first-pass metabolism. Why does it vary between individuals? (4 marks)\n(b) Explain how the rectal route partially avoids first-pass metabolism. (2 marks)\n(c) List four factors that determine the magnitude of first-pass metabolism for a given drug. (4 marks)", "modelAnswer": "a) First-pass metabolism: after oral absorption, drug passes through the portal vein to the liver before reaching systemic circulation. Hepatic enzymes metabolise a fraction → reduced oral bioavailability.\n\nIndividual variation due to:\n(1) Genetic polymorphisms: CYP2D6, CYP2C19, CYP2C9 variants affect enzyme activity\n(2) Hepatic blood flow: varies with age, disease, posture, food\n(3) Liver disease: cirrhosis reduces functional hepatocytes, portosystemic shunting\n(4) Enzyme induction/inhibition by co-administered drugs\n(5) Age: neonates have immature CYP450, elderly have reduced hepatic mass\n\nb) The lower rectum is drained by the middle and inferior rectal veins → systemic circulation (internal iliac vein) → bypasses portal vein. Upper rectum drains into superior rectal vein → portal vein. So ~50% of a rectally administered drug may bypass first-pass metabolism. This is incomplete and variable.\n\nc) Factors determining first-pass magnitude:\n(1) HEPATIC EXTRACTION RATIO (E): drugs with high E (e.g., lidocaine 0.7, propranolol 0.6) undergo extensive first-pass → low oral F.\n(2) ENZYME CAPACITY: saturation at higher doses can reduce first-pass (e.g., propranolol shows dose-dependent F).\n(3) GUT WALL METABOLISM: CYP3A4 in enterocytes contributes significantly (e.g., midazolam, felodipine).\n(4) TRANSPORTERS: P-gp efflux back into gut lumen reduces absorption and increases effective first-pass.\n(5) BLOOD FLOW: high hepatic blood flow increases delivery to hepatocytes → greater extraction."},
                    {"stem": "(a) Describe the clinical features and management of phenytoin toxicity. (4 marks)\n(b) How would you manage a patient with phenytoin level of 28 µg/mL who has nystagmus, ataxia, and slurred speech? (3 marks)\n(c) What factors can precipitate phenytoin toxicity in a previously stable patient? (3 marks)", "modelAnswer": "a) Phenytoin toxicity features (dose-dependent):\n- Level 10-20: therapeutic\n- Level 20-30: nystagmus on lateral gaze\n- Level 30-40: ataxia, slurred speech, blurred vision\n- Level >40: lethargy, confusion, coma, respiratory depression\n- Chronic toxicity: cognitive impairment, peripheral neuropathy, cerebellar atrophy\n\nb) Management of toxicity (phenytoin 28 + symptoms):\n(1) STOP phenytoin\n(2) Check level, electrolytes, albumin, LFTs\n(3) Assess free phenytoin level (especially if hypoalbuminaemia)\n(4) Supportive care: airway protection if reduced GCS\n(5) Given saturable kinetics, level will decline slowly (t½ ~24-100h depending on how saturated)\n(6) Monitor level daily until <20 µg/mL\n(7) Restart at lower dose once symptoms resolved (e.g., 200 mg/day) with close monitoring\n(8) Haemodialysis is ineffective (high protein binding, large Vd)\n\nc) Precipitants of phenytoin toxicity:\n(1) DRUG INTERACTIONS (inhibitors): amiodarone, fluconazole, isoniazid, sulphonamides, cimetidine, valproate\n(2) HEPATIC IMPAIRMENT: reduced CYP2C9 activity\n(3) HYPOALBUMINAEMIA: increased free fraction (same total level → more toxicity)\n(4) RENAL FAILURE: uraemia displaces phenytoin from albumin\n(5) SMALL DOSE INCREASE: due to saturable kinetics, 30-50 mg increase can cause dramatic level rise\n(6) NON-COMPLIANCE followed by resumption: if partial non-compliance allows metabolism to recover, resuming the same dose may overshoot"},
                    {"stem": "(a) Explain the concept of steady-state concentration and how it is affected by changes in clearance. (4 marks)\n(b) A patient stabilised on warfarin with INR 2.5 develops heart failure. How might this affect warfarin requirements? (3 marks)\n(c) Why may it take several days for a new steady state to be reached after changing the dose of warfarin? (3 marks)", "modelAnswer": "a) Steady state (SS): rate of drug administration = rate of elimination → Cp remains stable within a dosing interval. Equation: Cp_ss = (F × dose/τ) / CL. Effect of clearance: If CL decreases (e.g., organ impairment, drug interaction), Cp_ss INCREASES for the same dose. If CL increases (induction), Cp_ss DECREASES. Time to SS depends only on half-life (5 t½), not on dose.\n\nb) Heart failure → reduced hepatic blood flow → reduced warfarin clearance → increased Cp_ss → INR rises → risk of bleeding. Additionally, HF causes hepatic congestion → further reduces CYP2C9 activity. Management: reduce warfarin dose, monitor INR more frequently (every 1-3 days), target INR 2-3.\n\nc) Warfarin's effect is not direct — it inhibits synthesis of clotting factors II, VII, IX, X by blocking vitamin K epoxide reductase. Existing clotting factors must be cleared before full anticoagulant effect is seen. Factor VII (t½ 6h) declines fastest → early PT prolongation. Factor II (t½ 60h) declines slowest → full effect takes 5-7 days. Similarly, after dose change, new steady state of factor levels takes several days to establish. Warfarin itself has t½ ~36-48h → 5 t½ = 7.5-10 days for the drug to reach SS."}
                ]
            },
            {
                "letter": "C",
                "name": "Long Answer Questions",
                "marks": 30,
                "questions": [
                    {"stem": "A 58-year-old man (85 kg) with a history of hypertension (amlodipine 10 mg daily), atrial fibrillation (warfarin), and type 2 diabetes (metformin 1 g BID) is prescribed clarithromycin 500 mg BID for a respiratory tract infection. Three days later, he presents with severe muscle pain, weakness, and dark urine. His investigations show: CK 45,000 U/L, serum creatinine 2.5 mg/dL (baseline 1.0), K+ 6.0 mmol/L, and his INR is 5.8 (target 2-3). His medications at home also include atorvastatin 40 mg daily (started 6 months ago).\n\na) Identify the most likely diagnosis and analyse all drug-drug interactions that contributed. Focus on CYP450 and transporter interactions. (10 marks)\nb) Explain the mechanism by which clarithromycin causes this interaction, including its effect on P-glycoprotein and CYP3A4. (6 marks)\nc) Outline the acute management of this patient's rhabdomyolysis, including management of hyperkalaemia and the elevated INR. (8 marks)\nd) What principles would guide future antibiotic selection in this patient to avoid similar interactions? (6 marks)", "modelAnswer": "a) Diagnosis: RHABDOMYOLYSIS due to clarithromycin-atorvastatin interaction, complicated by warfarin over-anticoagulation.\n\nDRUG INTERACTION ANALYSIS:\n\n(1) CLARITHROMYCIN + ATORVASTATIN (primary cause):\n- Clarithromycin is a POTENT CYP3A4 inhibitor AND P-glycoprotein inhibitor\n- Atorvastatin is a CYP3A4 substrate AND P-gp substrate\n- Clarithromycin → inhibits atorvastatin metabolism (CYP3A4) → atorvastatin levels ↑ 4-5×\n- Clarithromycin → inhibits atorvastatin efflux (P-gp) → further increases atorvastatin absorption and reduces biliary clearance\n- Result: massive atorvastatin accumulation → myotoxicity → rhabdomyolysis (CK 45,000)\n\n(2) CLARITHROMYCIN + WARFARIN:\n- Clarithromycin inhibits CYP3A4 AND CYP1A2 → reduces R-warfarin metabolism\n- Clarithromycin also reduces gut flora that produce vitamin K\n- Result: elevated INR (5.8) → bleeding risk\n\n(3) AMLODIPINE + CLARITHROMYCIN:\n- Amlodipine is a CYP3A4 substrate → levels increase → risk of hypotension, oedema\n- (Less clinically relevant here but contributes to overall drug burden)\n\n(4) RHABDOMYOLYSIS → ACUTE KIDNEY INJURY:\n- Myoglobinuria → ATN (acute tubular necrosis) → AKI (Cr 1.0 → 2.5)\n- AKI reduces clearance of renally eliminated drugs (metformin → risk of lactic acidosis)\n- Metformin: STOP due to AKI (risk of lactic acidosis)\n- Hyperkalaemia (6.0) due to AKI + rhabdomyolysis (K+ release from damaged muscle)\n\nb) Clarithromycin mechanism:\n\nCYP3A4 INHIBITION:\n- Clarithromycin is a MACROLIDE antibiotic that forms a CYP3A4-metabolite complex (mechanism-based inhibition).\n- It binds to the haem iron of CYP3A4, forming an inactive metabolic intermediate (MI) complex.\n- This is IRREVERSIBLE (until new enzyme synthesis).\n- Result: CYP3A4 activity in liver and gut is markedly reduced.\n- Atorvastatin normally undergoes extensive CYP3A4 metabolism → inhibition → levels increase 4-5×.\n\nP-GLYCOPROTEIN (P-gp) INHIBITION:\n- Clarithromycin also inhibits P-glycoprotein (ABCB1) in the gut, liver, and kidney.\n- In gut: increased absorption of oral P-gp substrates (atorvastatin).\n- In liver: reduced biliary excretion of atorvastatin.\n- In kidney: reduced tubular secretion.\n- Combined effect: atorvastatin levels may increase 8-10× with dual CYP3A4 + P-gp inhibition.\n\nThis dual inhibition mechanism is shared with other drugs: erythromycin, ketoconazole, ritonavir, ciclosporin. It explains why clarithromycin has more severe interactions than azithromycin (which does not inhibit CYP3A4).\n\nc) Acute management:\n\nSTEP 1: STOP ALL OFFENDING DRUGS\n- Stop clarithromycin, atorvastatin, warfarin, metformin, amlodipine\n\nSTEP 2: RHABDOMYOLYSIS MANAGEMENT\n- AGGRESSIVE IV FLUIDS: 0.9% NaCl at 200-300 mL/h (target urine output 2-3 mL/kg/h)\n- Monitor: urine output, CK, K+, Cr, Ca2+, PO4, uric acid\n- Consider: sodium bicarbonate if metabolic acidosis (alkalinises urine → prevents myoglobin precipitation)\n- Consider: mannitol if urine output insufficient despite fluids (but avoid if already AKI with oliguria)\n\nSTEP 3: HYPERKALAEMIA MANAGEMENT (K+ 6.0)\n- ECG monitoring for T-wave changes, widened QRS\n- CALCIUM GLUCONATE 10%: 10-30 mL IV over 2-5 min (cardioprotection)\n- INSULIN + GLUCOSE: 10 U regular insulin + 50 mL 50% dextrose IV\n- SALBUTAMOL: 10-20 mg nebulised (shifts K+ into cells)\n- SODIUM BICARBONATE: 1 mEq/kg IV if acidotic\n- Consider: calcium resonium or patiromer (oral binders)\n- Consider: haemodialysis if K+ >6.5 refractory to medical therapy, or if AKI severe\n\nSTEP 4: INR MANAGEMENT (5.8)\n- If no active bleeding: hold warfarin, give vitamin K 2.5 mg orally\n- If minor bleeding: vitamin K 5 mg IV slow + monitor\n- If major bleeding: prothrombin complex concentrate (PCC) 25-50 U/kg + vitamin K 10 mg IV\n- Daily INR monitoring\n\nSTEP 5: MONITORING\n- CK q6-12h until peak then decline\n- K+, Cr, Ca2+, PO4, uric acid, LFTs\n- Urine output, colour (myoglobinuria → tea-coloured urine)\n- Cardiac monitoring (hyperkalaemia)\n\nSTEP 6: RENAL REPLACEMENT THERAPY\n- Indications: refractory hyperkalaemia, severe metabolic acidosis, fluid overload, uraemic symptoms\n- Intermittent haemodialysis or CVVH\n\nd) Principles for future antibiotic selection:\n\n(1) AVOID POTENT CYP3A4 INHIBITORS:\n- Avoid: clarithromycin, erythromycin, ketoconazole, itraconazole, HIV protease inhibitors\n- Safe alternatives for respiratory infections:\n   - AZITHROMYCIN: macrolide but does NOT inhibit CYP3A4 (no significant drug interactions)\n   - DOXYCYCLINE: no CYP3A4 effect\n   - AMOXICILLIN: no CYP interactions\n   - LEVOFLOXACIN/MOXIFLOXACIN: minimal CYP inhibition (but moxifloxacin: QT prolongation caution)\n\n(2) CONSIDER NARROW-SPECTRUM AGENTS FIRST:\n- Penicillins, cephalosporins — safe with statins\n\n(3) IF MACROLIDE NEEDED:\n- Azithromycin is the safest macrolide in this setting (no CYP3A4 or P-gp inhibition)\n\n(4) ANTICOAGULATION MANAGEMENT DURING ANTIBIOTICS:\n- Increase INR monitoring frequency\n- Consider temporarily reducing warfarin dose when starting CYP-inhibiting antibiotics\n- Or consider short-term LMWH bridge if warfarin must be held\n\n(5) ANTIHYPERTENSIVES:\n- Amlodipine: CYP3A4 substrate. If clarithromycin MUST be used, monitor for hypotension/oedema\n- Consider: amlodipine dose reduction during the antibiotic course\n\n(6) PATIENT COUNSELLING:\n- Report: muscle pain, weakness, dark urine (rhabdomyolysis warning)\n- Report: bruising, bleeding (INR warning)"},
                    {"stem": "A 32-year-old woman (55 kg) with newly diagnosed epilepsy (focal seizures) is started on phenytoin. She has a history of Crohn disease with short bowel syndrome (status post-ileal resection). She is on oral contraceptives and takes regular vitamin D and calcium supplements. After 2 weeks on phenytoin 300 mg daily, she has continued seizures. Her total phenytoin level is 4.5 µg/mL. Her albumin is 3.8 g/dL (normal). Her Crohn disease is in remission, but she continues to have 2-3 loose stools daily.\n\na) Identify all the factors that may contribute to this patient's subtherapeutic phenytoin level despite an adequate dose. Consider absorption, metabolism, and drug interactions. (8 marks)\nb) Calculate the appropriate loading dose of phenytoin for this patient (Vd = 0.65 L/kg, target Cp = 15 µg/mL). Include administration precautions. (6 marks)\nc) Discuss the interaction between phenytoin and oral contraceptives. What advice would you give this patient? (6 marks)\nd) Phenytoin may affect vitamin D metabolism. Explain the mechanism and what you would monitor in this patient who already has Crohn disease. (4 marks)\ne) If phenytoin cannot achieve therapeutic levels due to malabsorption, which alternative anticonvulsant would you recommend and why? (6 marks)", "modelAnswer": "a) Factors contributing to subtherapeutic phenytoin level:\n\n(1) MALABSORPTION (most likely primary cause):\n- Short bowel syndrome (ileal resection) → reduced absorptive surface area\n- Crohn disease → inflamed mucosa may impair absorption even in remission\n- Rapid GI transit (2-3 loose stools daily) → reduced contact time\n- Phenytoin is a weak acid with pH-dependent solubility → altered GI pH may affect dissolution\n\n(2) ENTEROHEPATIC RECIRCULATION: Phenytoin undergoes some enterohepatic cycling. Ileal resection interrupts bile acid reabsorption, potentially affecting drug recirculation.\n\n(3) DRUG INTERACTIONS:\n- Oral contraceptives: oestrogens can induce phenytoin metabolism (glucuronidation) → increased clearance → lower levels\n- Calcium supplements: Ca2+ can chelate phenytoin in the gut → reduced absorption (take at different times)\n\n(4) METABOLISM: Phenytoin follows saturable kinetics. At 300 mg/day for a 55 kg woman, the dose/kg (5.5 mg/kg) is at the lower end of the maintenance range (4-6 mg/kg). She may need higher mg/kg dosing.\n\n(5) NON-COMPLIANCE: Possible but less likely given active seizure management.\n\nb) Loading dose calculation:\n\nLD = Vd × target Cp / F\nVd = 0.65 L/kg × 55 kg = 35.75 L\ntarget Cp = 15 mg/L\nF (oral) = varies (but phenytoin F is 0.85-0.95 for capsules)\nLD = 35.75 × 15 / 0.92 = 583 mg approximately\n\nClinical approach: Give 600 mg oral phenytoin loading (divided into 2 doses of 300 mg, 2 hours apart, or 400 mg + 200 mg 2h apart).\n\nAlternatively: IV fosphenytoin loading:\n- Fosphenytoin 15-20 mg PE/kg IV (PE = phenytoin equivalents)\n- For 55 kg: fosphenytoin 825-1100 mg PE IV\n- Max rate: 150 mg PE/min (fosphenytoin is safer than IV phenytoin)\n\nPRECAUTIONS:\n- Oral phenytoin loading: monitor for GI upset, give with food if tolerated\n- IV phenytoin: max 50 mg/min, ECG/BP monitoring, NS only (not D5W), flush with NS before/after\n- IV fosphenytoin: max 150 mg PE/min, fewer infusion reactions\n\nc) Phenytoin ↔ oral contraceptives interaction:\n\nPHENYTOIN PERPETRATOR: Phenytoin induces CYP3A4 AND glucuronyl transferases → INCREASED oestrogen and progestin metabolism → DECREASED contraceptive efficacy → risk of contraceptive failure and pregnancy.\n\nBIDIRECTIONAL: Some evidence that oestrogens can INCREASE phenytoin metabolism → further decrease phenytoin levels.\n\nADVICE:\n(1) Use a HIGHER-DOSE oral contraceptive (e.g., 50 µg ethinyloestradiol instead of 30-35 µg) — but may not fully compensate.\n(2) BETTER: use a NON-HORMONAL contraceptive method:\n   - Copper IUD (most effective, no interaction)\n   - Barrier methods (condom, diaphragm) — less reliable\n   - Progestin-only methods may also be affected (implants, POP)\n(3) If hormonal contraception is essential: use a IUS (Mirena) — local progestin, less affected by enzyme induction.\n(4) Discuss the risk of contraceptive failure explicitly.\n(5) If the patient becomes pregnant despite contraception: phenytoin is teratogenic (foetal hydantoin syndrome) — discuss folic acid 5 mg daily.\n(6) Consider switching to a non-inducing anticonvulsant.\n\nd) Phenytoin and vitamin D:\n\nMECHANISM: Phenytoin is a CYP450 inducer that increases the hepatic metabolism of vitamin D (25-hydroxylation and subsequent breakdown). This leads to:\n- Reduced 25-hydroxyvitamin D levels\n- Reduced calcium absorption\n- Secondary hyperparathyroidism\n- Osteomalacia (in adults), rickets (in children)\n\nThis patient already has Crohn disease → baseline risk of vitamin D deficiency (malabsorption). Phenytoin further compounds this.\n\nMONITORING:\n(1) Vitamin D levels: 25-hydroxyvitamin D at baseline and q6-12 months\n(2) Calcium, phosphate, alkaline phosphatase, PTH\n(3) Bone mineral density (DEXA scan) if long-term therapy\n\nSUPPLEMENTATION: Vitamin D 800-2000 IU daily (or higher if deficient). Ensure adequate calcium intake.\n\ne) Alternative anticonvulsant recommendations:\n\nGiven malabsorption (short bowel + Crohn disease) and drug interactions (contraceptives), the ideal anticonvulsant should:\n- Have good oral absorption independent of GI factors\n- Have minimal protein binding\n- Have linear kinetics\n- Not interact with oral contraceptives\n- Not affect vitamin D metabolism\n\nBEST CHOICE: LEVETIRACETAM\n- Absorption: Rapid and complete (oral bioavailability >95%), minimal GI dependency\n- Protein binding: <10% → not affected by hypoalbuminaemia or displacement\n- Kinetics: Linear, predictable\n- Metabolism: 66% renally excreted unchanged, 24% hydrolysed (non-CYP)\n- Drug interactions: NONE — does not induce CYP450 → safe with oral contraceptives, no effect on vitamin D\n- No need for TDM (wide therapeutic index)\n- Dose: 500-1000 mg BID\n- Caution: psychiatric side effects (rare), adjust for renal function\n\nGOOD ALTERNATIVE: LACOSAMIDE\n- Linear kinetics\n- Minimal protein binding (<15%)\n- No CYP induction\n- Efficacy in focal seizures\n- Dose: 50-400 mg/day (titrate)\n\nALTERNATIVE: GABAPENTIN\n- No metabolism, renally excreted\n- No drug interactions\n- But: less effective for focal seizures than levetiracetam\n- Dose: 300-1200 mg TID (requires renal adjustment)\n\nAVOID:\n- Carbamazepine, phenobarbitone, primidone: CYP inducers (interact with contraceptives, vitamin D)\n- Lamotrigine: absorption may be affected by GI disease; slow titration\n- Valproate: high protein binding, nonlinear kinetics, interacts with contraceptives\n\nRECOMMENDATION: Switch to levetiracetam 500 mg BID. Monitor seizure control, renal function. Discuss switching to copper IUD or continuing contraception with awareness of reduced efficacy."}
                ]
            }
        ]
    }
}